//chrome - Chromium's own browser, with tabs and an omnibox - on a Linux
family platform without Linux's kernel, D-Bus, NSS or a crash handler.

content_shell is the embedder the tree ships to test //content with: four
buttons and an address field. What people mean by Chromium is //chrome, and
it builds from the same checkout, the same args.gn and every patch before
this one. A -k 0 build of //chrome:chrome failed in 21 places, all crashpad;
with crashpad gated it failed in 18 more, then linked with 7 undefined
symbols, then with 2 relocation errors. Every one of them is a seam this
series has drawn before, in a directory it had not reached.

A CRASH HANDLER THAT IS NOT THERE

content_shell met crashpad's Linux half (ptrace, PR_SET_DUMPABLE,
/proc/<pid>/task) in patch 0058 and answered it in content/shell. //chrome
reaches it from a dozen places, so the answer goes where the library is:
components/crash/core/app says has_crash_handler, and a buildflag
HAS_CRASH_HANDLER says it to the preprocessor. The library keeps its switches
and its client interface; crashpad.cc and the handler data dependency go.
Every caller asks the one question: chrome_main_delegate's
InitializeCrashpad, the crash-signal socket a child is handed (-1, which
already means "none"), the upload list (the JavaScript error log is the half
that is left), the enterprise crash reporting context (ChromeOS already had
no crashpad database there), SetUploadConsent, and the metrics service's
system-profile stream.

Two things follow from there being no crashpad. V8's WebAssembly trap
handler is installed by crashpad as its first-chance handler on Linux, so
content_renderer_client.cc lets V8 install its own - what content_shell does
whenever no crash handler is there. And chrome/common/crash_keys.cc read a
crashpad annotation's value back to pass it to child processes; without
crashpad the wrapper keeps the value itself.

NO D-BUS, AND FOUR FILES THAT HAD NOT HEARD

use_dbus = false is in this build's args.gn (content, M165). //chrome had
not been built that way on Linux and it shows:

  - platform_util_linux.cc was compiled only with D-Bus, and its
    ShowItemInFolder and OpenExternal were the only definitions on Linux. It
    already falls back to xdg-open and xdg-email when neither the portal nor
    FileManager1 answers; without a bus, that fallback is the answer.
  - notifications: the only system bridge on Linux is the freedesktop
    service. HAS_SYSTEM_NOTIFICATION_BRIDGE in notification_platform_bridge.h
    says whether Create() exists; without one, every notification goes to
    the message center - which is what Chrome falls back to when a system
    bridge fails anyway.
  - BlueZ: PostCreateMainMessageLoop initialised BluezDBusManager under
    USE_DBUS and PostDestroyThreads shut it down unconditionally. The
    Bluetooth-stack metric (BlueZ or Floss, both over D-Bus) follows
    device/bluetooth's use_bluez default.
  - dynamic_launcher_portal.cc is an XDG portal and has no callers; it
    follows use_dbus the way its dependency already did.
  - ui::GlobalAcceleratorListener::GetInstance fell off the end of a
    function returning a value when neither ChromeOS nor D-Bus applied.
    clang compiles that to ud2, and the browser stopped on it with SIGILL.

NO NSS

use_nss_* = false is in args.gn too (M162). On Linux, NSS's database IS the
platform client-certificate store and the platform root store's user-added
certificates:

  - the certificate manager's platform-root-store half follows
    net/cert/internal/system_trust_store.h's own condition (patch 0049 drew
    it for services/cert_verifier) - is_platform_root_store_supported in the
    mojom, CERTIFICATE_MANAGER_HAS_PLATFORM_ROOT_STORE in C++, and the same
    expression in the WebUI's <if> - so the page has no "imported from your
    operating system" section to pretend with, and keeps the Chrome Root Store
    and user-added certificates, which need no platform store;
  - the client-certificate half on Linux without NSS is the read-only source
    with nothing behind it that every platform without a store gets;
  - ProfileNetworkContextService's client cert store is the empty store,
    wrapped the way Android's is, so the profile's and the browser's own
    stores still apply;
  - Firefox password import needs an NSS to decrypt with, and
    FIREFOX_PASSWORDS_ARE_IMPORTABLE says so in the one header both the
    importer and the importer list include - macOS was already the platform
    with none, and importer_list.cc's own comment says why the offer must not
    be made: "don't promise an import service that can't be delivered".

LINUX'S KERNEL, ONCE MORE

  - power_metrics' only Linux energy provider is perf_event_open(2), and
    SystemPowerMonitor CHECKs that it has one, so both follow HAS_LINUX_KERNEL.
  - discovery_network_list_posix.cc identifies a network by the hardware
    address getifaddrs reports as AF_PACKET, and SIOCGIWESSID is a
    wireless-extensions ioctl. Without either, each running interface is
    reported with an empty id - ComputeNetworkId's kNetworkIdUnknown, "we
    can't determine the set of networks" - rather than an empty list, which
    would be kNetworkIdDisconnected and false.
  - chrome://sandbox reports SandboxLinux's bits. None of them is here, and
    every answer is no, which makes the page's own verdict the true one.
  - memory_details.cc includes zygote_host_linux.h under USE_ZYGOTE and calls
    it under a platform list; it asks USE_ZYGOTE both times now.
  - ChromeBrowserMainExtraPartsViews refuses to run as root "so that we don't
    destroy the profile" and because of the setuid sandbox - both Linux's.
    A platform in the family without that kernel can have one principal,
    uid 0 because there is no other, and no other user to ruin a profile for.

THE WEAK TLS INIT FUNCTION, THIRD AND FOURTH TIME

M155 (V8, patch 0033) and patch 0056 (ANGLE) found this: an extern
thread_local read from another translation unit, with no initialiser in
sight, is read through a weak TLS init function that the defining unit never
emits - so the call is to absolute zero, which a PC-relative branch reaches
only from an image near zero. This platform loads programs at 512 GiB. re2's
hooks::context and openscreen's ScopedTraceOperation::traces_ and root_node_
are all nullptr-initialised; constinit says so where every reader can see it.

WHAT IS NOT HERE

No sandbox and no crash handler arrive with any of this, and the binary is
checked for their absence: no crashpad::, no InitializeCrashpad, no
SystemPowerMonitor. The word lean_os appears nowhere below.

